Pseudocode:

```
func (asynQueryable) Querier(....) storage.Querier {
  return new AsyncQuerier{} 
}

func (AsyncQuerier) Select(...) (set, w, err) {
  ret <- chan
  go func() {
     ret <- Select
  }
   return AsyncIterator{ret: ret}, nil, err
}

func (a AsyncIterator) Next() bool { 
   set <- a.resp
   // Iterate over set
}
```

With this we achieve full concurrency control. :heart: 

Is this what you thought about @brian-brazil ? The only downsides is lack of warning propagation... but that's not super critical I guess.

@brian-brazil @bwplotka From the discussions here and IRC, I understood it is acceptable to change `storage.Querier` interface (which was the original implementation).
But I think I now understand what was wrong with it, as @brian-brazil has pointed out in https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/pull/7198#discussion_r419337221.

I'm going to get rid of select manager and embed behaviour into Querier.  I'm going to try to split `.Select`s into a two-phased action. 

Also I would like to combine this approach with a lazy iterator/set pattern that @bwplotka suggested, to also get rid of the closure that wraps `.Select`s in PromQL engine.

To achieve that what do you think having an interface like below?

```diff
// Querier provides querying access over time-series data of a fixed time range.
type Querier interface {
	baseQuerier

	// Select returns a set of series that matches the given label matches.
	// For async .Select this would return a lazy iterator.
	// Can return scheduling errors, we can also keep Warnings.
-	Select(sortSeries bool, hints *SelectHints, matchers ...*labels.Matcher) (SeriesSet, Warnings, error)
+	Select(sortSeries bool, hints *SelectHints, matchers ...*labels.Matcher) (SeriesSet, error) 

+	// Exec returns aggregated warnings and errors occurred while executing scheduled Selects for a given Query.
+	Exec() (Warnings, error)
}
```

This implementation would require a minimum set of changes to TSDB implementation of Querier.
And also without touching underlying implementation `storage.Fanout` can wrap remote read querier for concurrent queries, if necessary.
 
What do you think?
